===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} *{{term||tilȝer, *telȝer|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|telgor|lang=ang}}, {{term|telgra|lang=ang}}, {{term|telgre|lang=ang}} {{gloss|"twig, branch, shoot"}} (also {{term|telga|lang=ang}}, {{term|telge|lang=ang}} (whence {{l|en|tillow}})), from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*telgô}}, {{m|gem-pro|*telgōn||twig, branch}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*delgʰ-||to split, divide, cut, carve}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|telg||descendant, scion, offshoot, shoot|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|nds-nl|-}} {{term|telge||twig, branch|lang=nds-nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Zelge||twig, branch, bough|lang=de}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|telning||branch, scion, sapling|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|tág||willow-twig|lang=is}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|tillow}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A young [[tree]].
#: {{rfquotek|Evelyn}}
# A [[shoot]] of a [[plant]] which springs from the root or bottom of the original [[stalk]]; a [[sapling]]; a [[sucker]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To put forth new [[shoot]]s from the root or from around the bottom of the original stalk; stool.

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|put forth new shoots}}
* Italian: {{t|it|accestire}}
{{trans-mid}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 3===
{{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|telier||beam used in weaving|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{term|telarium|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|tela||web|lang=la}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|archery|lang=en}} The [[stock]]; a beam on a crossbow carved to fit the [[arrow]], or the point of balance in a longbow.
#* Beaumont and Fletcher
#*: You can shoot in a '''tiller'''.
# {{context|nautical|lang=en}} A [[bar]] of iron or wood connected with the [[rudderhead]] and [[leadline]], usually [[forward]], in which the [[rudder]] is moved as desired by the tiller (FM 55-501).
# {{context|nautical|lang=en}} The handle of the [[rudder]] which the [[helmsman]] holds to [[steer]] the boat, a piece of wood or metal extending forward from the rudder over or through the [[transom]]. Generally attached at the top of the rudder.
# A [[handle]]; a [[stalk]].
# {{context|UK|dialect|obsolete|lang=en}} A small [[drawer]]; a [[till]].
#: {{rfquotek|Dryden}}

